-doesNotRecognize:xxx is handy for "proxy" objects.  Proxy objects are
used to re-route messages.  For example, class "Foo" objects exist on
a separate machine and can only be reached through inter-machine
messages (eg: UDP/IP).  You program has class "Bar" objects that
accept all messages acceptable to class Foo objects.  A message sent
to a Bar object is converted into an IP datagram, forwarded to the
remote machine, and munged by the appropriate Foo object.

The -doesNotRecognize method makes the trick of forwarding messages
from one object to another simple because the proxy class (in this
case "Bar") does not have to implement any of the methods of the
target class ("Foo").  The Bar class overrides -doesNotRecognize.  The
implementation of this method takes the selector and its arguments,
and calls then calls the appropriate instance of the foo object.

eg:

-doesNotRecognize:aSel with:arg
{
	return [remoteObject perform:aSel with:arg];
}


			**** UNFORTUNATELY ****

One cannot use this trick in Objective-C because -doesNotRecognize
does not pass the arguments to the method orginally called, unlike
smalltalk.  There probably exists a paper or two on this someplace,
but I do not have a reference or two handy.  

Hope this helps!

